hing_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"**What the grids are made of.** Every grid is black, with a few single-pixel dots. There are always two special \"centre\" dots: one blue (1) and one red (2). The other dots are orange (7) and green (3).\n\n**What changes.** In every output, each orange dot has jumped to sit right next to the blue dot. Each green dot has jumped to sit right next to the red dot. Nothing else moves.\n\nThe pairing is the same in all three train pairs (7 goes to 1, 3 goes to 2), so it can be written as a fixed lookup: `{7: 1, 3: 2}`.\n\n**Which neighbouring cell a dot lands in.** Every satellite dot already shares a row or a column with its centre. It slides along that line until it touches the centre, so it stops on the same side it came from. Pair 3 shows this:\n- The 7 at (6,1) shares row 6 with the 1 at (6,10). It comes from the left, so it lands at (6,9).\n- The 7 at (14,10) shares column 10 with the 1 and comes from below, so it lands at (7,10).\n- The 3s at (0,6), (11,14) and (14,6) come from above, the right and below the 2 at (11,6). They land at (10,6), (11,7) and (12,6).\n\nChecking this by hand against pairs 1 and 2 gives the same picture: up to four satellites, each ending in the plus-shaped ring around its centre. The program below is checked against all three train pairs.\n\n**Test input.** The 2 is at (3,11). Its 3s are at (0,11) above, (12,11) below and (3,0) to the left. They become (2,11), (4,11) and (3,10). The 1 is at (6,5). Its 7s are at (6,0) to the left and (6,14) to the right. They become (6,4) and (6,6).\n\n**Construction stages.**\n1. Erase all the satellites, leaving only the centres.\n2. Move each centre's satellites into place, one centre at a time.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    partner = {7: 1, 3: 2}\n    out = g.copy()\n    centres = {}\n    for c in set(partner.values()):\n        ys, xs = np.where(g == c)\n        if len(ys):\n            centres[c] = (int(ys[0]), int(xs[0]))\n    moves = []\n    for y, x in zip(*np.where(g != bg)):\n        col = int(g[y, x])\n        if col not in partner or partner[col] not in centres:\n            continue\n        cy, cx = centres[partner[col]]\n        if y == cy:\n            ny, nx = cy, cx + (1 if x > cx else -1)\n        elif x == cx:\n            ny, nx = cy + (1 if y > cy else -1), cx\n        else:\n            continue\n        out[y, x] = bg\n        moves.append((partner[col], ny, nx, col))\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Remove every satellite dot (7s and 3s), leaving only the centre dots 1 and 2.\")\n    for c in sorted(centres):\n        for cc, ny, nx, col in moves:\n            if cc == c:\n                out[ny, nx] = col\n        emit(out.copy(), f\"Slide each satellite aligned with centre colour {c} along its row/column until it touches the centre.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"It slides along that line until it touches the centre, so it stops on the same side it came from."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Move each centre's satellites into place, one centre at a time."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Slide each satellite aligned with centre colour 1 along its row/column until it touches the centre."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"449da5e22cfc11e7b9e6b3d6bb2bf13d20d08cad0556bb9f5bfbd396808aa08e","task_sha256":"227682aa679c25e927d58fdb6ba32df51ae86e7e907857cf5d990ff6fa104207","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
